    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tBefore 1969, John L. (1926-2016) and Sue (1928-2005) Tishman, New York (P23/P27) [1]; 2007, John and Sue Tishman sold to private collector, New York [2]; 2009, private collection, New York, gift to Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] The Tishmans assigned numbers to works in their collection, whereby lower-numbered objects were acquired prior to higher-numbered objects. The numbers changed over time as works were donated or sold from the collection. A series of appraisals of the collection produced by Robert Sonin, copies of which are in the curatorial file and date as early as 1970, list this object as P23 and P27. Another work on the list, numbered P31 and P35 in different versions, was exhibited and published in 1969, providing a terminus ante quem by which the proposed object was in the Tishman collection.\n\t<br />\n\t[2] As per letter from John L Tishman to private collection. Copy in curatorial file.\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"In the Andes the last millennium BCE witnessed an explosive increase in art production and a consistent set of visual motifs across a variety of media. Fierce, fanged creatures, blending the qualities of humans and predatory animals, appear in abundance, brandishing long claws. Because the style was traditionally associated with the ritual center of Chavín de Huantar, objects encountered across Peru that share these visual qualities have been described as Chavín style. Artists across Peru, however, adapted the Chavín style to their own contexts and needs.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA2_20-T6-1_CLA_FA.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
